<TopicPage slug="subjects/medicine/expert/health-systems-and-policy">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Health systems are <strong>the machinery between medical knowledge and actual health</strong>:
    financing, workforce, hospitals, information and governance. A brilliant drug in a broken
    system reaches no one. Comparative health policy is therefore not ideology — it is a natural
    experiment running continuously across 200 countries, with measurable outcomes.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the models, the trade-off triangle, and the leverage points" widget="equationStep" config={sysCfg} />

  <h2>What the comparisons show</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The US spends ~2× peer nations per capita</strong> with lower life expectancy — administrative costs, prices (drugs, procedures, salaries) rather than utilisation explain most of the gap (Anderson, Reinhardt research).</li>
    <li><strong>Single-payer vs multi-payer both work:</strong> the UK (tax) and Germany (insurers) achieve top outcomes at similar cost-per-outcome — the design details (primary-care strength, negotiated prices) matter more than the label.</li>
    <li><strong>Workforce is the binding constraint:</strong> the WHO projects a 10-million health-worker shortfall by 2030, concentrated where disease burden is highest — migration from poor to rich countries is health policy's quiet crisis.</li>
    <li><strong>COVID as systems stress test:</strong> the same virus produced 10× different death rates across comparable countries — preparedness, trust and leadership measurably mattered. Health policy earned its budget arguments the hard way.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> WHO Global Health Expenditure Database; Commonwealth Fund international comparisons; Starfield, Primary Care; Papanicolas & Jha, JAMA (health spending analyses).</p>
</TopicPage>
